              "index": 11,
              "bbox": [
                0.084,
                0.905,
                0.251,
                0.92
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 12.12: Cone of pine"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.299,
            0.088,
            0.589,
            0.107
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 12.6: Let us explore"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.309,
            0.109,
            0.889,
            0.145
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. Observe some bryophytes with the help of a hand lens. Collect them in a watch glass."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.308,
            0.147,
            0.89,
            0.2
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. Put a drop of water on it and observe it under a dissecting microscope. A dissecting microscope enables us to view magnified images of live specimens."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.298,
            0.201,
            0.889,
            0.237
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "How do they "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "differ",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " from the leaves that you usually observe in plants in your surroundings?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.296,
            0.239,
            0.89,
            0.308
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Bryophytes require water for reproduction. Therefore, they are called the ‘amphibians’ of the plant kingdom. They show how plants began to colonise land, while remaining dependent on moisture."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.297,
            0.316,
            0.888,
            0.354
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Pteridophyta — Adaptation to land and having structural transport system"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.296,
            0.356,
            0.89,
            0.594
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Look at Fig. 12.11a. What changes do you observe in the plant structure as compared to thallophytes and bryophytes? Pteridophytes (pteris derived from pteron means feather and phyton means plant), such as ferns, possess true roots, stems and leaves. How do you think that the transport of water and food takes place in all parts of these plants? One possible explanation is that ferns have specialised transport tissue. How can you test this explanation? A cross section of the stem of a fern can be cut and seen under a microscope. You may also observe a permanent slide of a cross section of a fern stem available in your school laboratory. The cross section shows vascular tissues, xylem and phloem, which transport water and food, respectively, throughout the plant (Fig. 12.11b). Pteridophytes require aquatic conditions for reproduction and do not produce seeds."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.299,
            0.601,
            0.601,
            0.62
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 12.7: Let us compare"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.309,
            0.622,
            0.888,
            0.657
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. Recall the cross section of the sunflower stem you have studied in Chapter 3 (Fig. 3.7)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 21,
          "bbox": [
            0.31,
            0.659,
            0.889,
            0.712
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. Compare the cross section of the stem of a fern, a pteridophytic plant (Fig. 12.11) with a cross section of sunflower stem you studied in Chapter 3 (Fig. 3.7)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 22,
          "bbox": [
            0.308,
            0.714,
            0.891,
            0.767
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. What difference do you observe in the vascular tissue of the fern stem and of the stem of higher plants? Write your observations. Share and discuss them in class."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 23,
          "bbox": [
            0.299,
            0.775,
            0.772,
            0.794
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Gymnosperms — Reproduction without water"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 24,
          "bbox": [
            0.296,
            0.796,
            0.89,
            0.917
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Gymnosperms (gymnos means naked and spermos means seed), such as pines (Fig. 12.12) and cycads are well-adapted to cold and dry regions. Their needle-like or scale-like leaves reduce water loss and withstand harsh conditions. They produce seeds, which protect the developing embryo and contain stored food. Gymnosperms do not require aquatic conditions for fertilisation. Their seeds are not enclosed in fruits and are often exposed on cones."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
